+# Narrowing the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is Admitted into Scope
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian out of scope — only owners are searchable by name. A request then arrived to narrow the veterinarian directory to a named specialty, on both the page and its machine-readable form.
+
+That request tests the NG-9 boundary. Filtering the directory by an attribute it already publishes is not the free-text search NG-9 declined, yet it touches the same row. Whether the row admits it or forbids it was left open by the survey-derived provenance the Non-Goals preamble records.
+
+The owner has now decided (2026-08-24).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Read it as free-text vet search and decline under NG-9.** Rejected: specialty narrowing filters data the directory already displays; it adds no new free-text surface.
+2. **Drop NG-9 and open free-text vet search.** Rejected: the NG-9 rationale holds for free text — repeating owner-style search over free-form input elsewhere adds surface, not understanding.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** (chosen): admit attribute-filtering of the already-published directory; keep free-text vet search out.
+
+## Decision
+
+Narrowing the veterinarian directory to a named specialty is in scope, delivered as REQ-VET-003. The narrowing constrains the specialties the directory already publishes, on both the page and its machine-readable form, matching the whole specialty name and ignoring letter case.
+
+Free-text search for a veterinarian — or a pet or a visit — stays out. The distinction is the data. REQ-VET-003 filters a collection the directory already shows; free-text search would add a new query surface over free-form input, which is what NG-9 declines.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 keeps its residual: free-text vet, pet, and visit search stay out. Only the specialty narrowing of the vet directory is admitted.
+- The machine-readable veterinarian list, withdrawn as REQ-VET-002, is reinstated as a supported surface serving REQ-VET-003. Its former "serves no requirement" defect is retired.
+- Whether a visible filter control or multi-specialty narrowing follows is deferred to separate requirements, tracked in the PRD's Open Questions.
+- Narrowing NG-9 further is a recorded owner decision with its own non-goal ADR, per the Non-Goals convention.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row.
+- [REQ-VET-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the requirement delivering the admitted narrowing.
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the VetController and VetRepository cells realizing the narrowing.
